WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.540
<v Mike>Super Mario Bros.

00:00:01.800 --> 00:00:03.680
<v Mike>Duck Hunt World Class Track Meet.

00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:06.740
<v Mike>You'll have to think fast and move even faster.

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:15.280
<v Mike>Your trusty hunting dog wades into the marshes to flush out your prey in this action-packed game that turns your home into an exciting stadium.

00:00:31.540 --> 00:00:39.520
<v Mike>And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:41.360
<v Hosts>I'm Mike. I'm Sean.

00:00:41.740 --> 00:00:42.760
<v Hosts>And I'm Joe.

00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:49.480
<v Mike>And we mean every game released in North America, even if we've covered Super Mario Bros.

00:00:49.580 --> 00:00:51.900
<v Mike>now three times on this show.

00:00:52.460 --> 00:00:54.960
<v Mike>Last time it was with just Duck Hunt.

00:00:55.120 --> 00:00:57.460
<v Mike>This time they've added World Class Track Meet.

00:00:57.460 --> 00:01:04.740
<v Mike>Before we talk about the reasoning for this or whatever, can we just quickly get out of the way how we still feel about these games?

00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:08.920
<v Mike>Anybody want to like controversially take Super Mario Bros.

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:10.140
<v Mike>off the essential games list?

00:01:11.080 --> 00:01:16.580
<v Sean>Good, good, and I don't really remember to be honest.

00:01:16.580 --> 00:01:28.580
<v Joe>Yeah, I never liked any of these like Track Meet or Track Meet clone type things, but Duck Hunt is still probably the game that I play in real life the most from the NES.

00:01:28.900 --> 00:01:30.540
<v Joe>Because it's just so easy to boot up.

00:01:30.660 --> 00:01:32.240
<v Joe>I've got it. I got my CRT here.

00:01:32.660 --> 00:01:35.880
<v Joe>Whenever I'm booting up the CRT, I'm like, well, I got to play a little bit of Duck Hunt.

00:01:36.280 --> 00:01:37.780
<v Sean>You boot up a CRT, right?

00:01:38.480 --> 00:01:40.400
<v Mike>Yeah, sure. You can power on.

00:01:40.820 --> 00:01:41.800
<v Joe>No, I boot it up.

00:01:41.940 --> 00:01:44.060
<v Sean>It goes through like a whole like Windows.

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:45.540
<v Mike>And then connect it to the internet?

00:01:45.540 --> 00:01:47.440
<v Mike>Yeah, yeah. Okay.

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:54.360
<v Mike>The funny thing about not remembering World Class Track Meet is that it's also technically the third time we're talking about this game.

00:01:54.360 --> 00:02:03.780
<v Mike>Because it was originally, you know, a niche little video game called Stadium Events, which now sells for like $25,000 complete in box.

00:02:03.940 --> 00:02:09.000
<v Mike>Before it was rebranded and removed from shelves as World Class Track Meet.

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:16.820
<v Sean>Yeah, the whole Stadium Events controversy really needed to take that name off of it after what happened.

00:02:17.360 --> 00:02:20.820
<v Mike>Right. There will be no events in stadiums.

00:02:21.100 --> 00:02:26.040
<v Mike>Damn. But obviously, if you haven't made the connection yet, Super Mario Bros.

00:02:26.140 --> 00:02:27.700
<v Mike>Duck Hunt World Class Track Meet.

00:02:27.900 --> 00:02:28.760
<v Mike>It's a compilation.

00:02:28.940 --> 00:02:30.960
<v Mike>It's all in one cart, which is pretty impressive.

00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:44.020
<v Mike>But it's a compilation because it was a pack-in game for a new NES bundle that included two controllers, an NES, a zapper, and a power pad.

00:02:44.020 --> 00:02:47.000
<v Mike>So with the previous set of Super Mario Bros.

00:02:47.340 --> 00:02:51.920
<v Mike>and Duck Hunt, you were getting a zapper with it, but you weren't getting the power pad.

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:54.540
<v Mike>And that was known as the action set.

00:02:54.700 --> 00:03:00.940
<v Mike>What would you call this new set that has the power pad included?

00:03:01.760 --> 00:03:03.140
<v Sean>The clearance set.

00:03:04.420 --> 00:03:06.580
<v Joe>Yeah, I'm trying to think because action, I get it.

00:03:06.680 --> 00:03:09.260
<v Joe>The gun, it's like an action movie, a lot of guns.

00:03:09.460 --> 00:03:17.000
<v Joe>But I feel like literally the power pad is probably the most action-based peripheral you can have.

00:03:17.140 --> 00:03:21.360
<v Joe>This has to be like they just have too many power pads and they've got a clean house.

00:03:22.300 --> 00:03:23.560
<v Mike>Right. It is a Nintendo.

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:28.340
<v Mike>That is another part of the reasoning is that if you remember, it was the Family Fun Fitness pad

00:03:28.340 --> 00:03:34.240
<v Mike>before Nintendo basically bought the rights to it and put the Nintendo branding all over it

00:03:34.240 --> 00:03:40.040
<v Mike>and rebranded it to the power pad because, of course, with the NES and Nintendo, now you're playing with power.

00:03:40.360 --> 00:03:45.620
<v Mike>And so giving it away, it is the power set we are talking about.

00:03:46.120 --> 00:03:47.980
<v Sean>Hmm. Now you're kicking with gas.

00:03:48.600 --> 00:03:56.560
<v Mike>Yep. And the power set retailed for $179, which is $455 in 2026.

00:03:56.560 --> 00:04:00.460
<v Mike>So, I don't know, not unfair.

